{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"7f862090de0dd7e4","name":"card_event_national_service_home","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/Homefront/events/card_event_national_service_home","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"NATIONAL SERVICE","text":"For each British card in your deck, give it +2 defense if a unit, otherwise reduce the cost by 1.","flavorText":"Britain’s National Service during WWII extended far beyond the battlefield. The National Service (Armed Forces) Act, passed in 1939, introduced conscription for men aged 18 to 41, and it was later expanded to include younger and older groups. From 1941, unmarried women aged 20 to 30 were also called up. While many conscripts served in the military, others were directed into essential civilian roles.The Bevin Boys were among those assigned to industrial work. Named after Minister of Labour Ernest Bevin, these young men were randomly selected to work in coal mines instead of joining the armed forces. Their labor was critical to keeping Britain’s war industries running but earned little recognition at the time. Women were conscripted into services like the Auxiliary Territorial Service (ATS), where they served as clerks, drivers, mechanics and radar operators. Others joined the Women’s Royal Naval Service or the Air Transport Auxiliary. National Service also directed thousands into factories, farms, and shipyards, filling shortages in vital industries. Whether in uniform or overalls, conscripts formed the backbone of Britain's wartime manpower, helping sustain the war effort on all fronts.","faction":"Britain","type":"order","rarity":"Unique","cost":5,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_national_service_home","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_national_service_home_title","SourceString":"NATIONAL SERVICE","LocalizedString":"NATIONAL SERVICE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":5,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_national_service_home_text","SourceString":"For each British card in your deck, give it +2 defense if a unit, otherwise reduce the cost by 1.","LocalizedString":"For each British card in your deck, give it +2 defense if a unit, otherwise reduce the cost by 1."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_national_service_home_history","SourceString":"Britain’s National Service during WWII extended far beyond the battlefield. The National Service (Armed Forces) Act, passed in 1939, introduced conscription for men aged 18 to 41, and it was later expanded to include younger and older groups. From 1941, unmarried women aged 20 to 30 were also called up. While many conscripts served in the military, others were directed into essential civilian roles.The Bevin Boys were among those assigned to industrial work. Named after Minister of Labour Ernest Bevin, these young men were randomly selected to work in coal mines instead of joining the armed forces. Their labor was critical to keeping Britain’s war industries running but earned little recognition at the time. Women were conscripted into services like the Auxiliary Territorial Service (ATS), where they served as clerks, drivers, mechanics and radar operators. Others joined the Women’s Royal Naval Service or the Air Transport Auxiliary. National Service also directed thousands into factories, farms, and shipyards, filling shortages in vital industries. Whether in uniform or overalls, conscripts formed the backbone of Britain's wartime manpower, helping sustain the war effort on all fronts.","LocalizedString":"Britain’s National Service during WWII extended far beyond the battlefield. The National Service (Armed Forces) Act, passed in 1939, introduced conscription for men aged 18 to 41, and it was later expanded to include younger and older groups. From 1941, unmarried women aged 20 to 30 were also called up. While many conscripts served in the military, others were directed into essential civilian roles.The Bevin Boys were among those assigned to industrial work. Named after Minister of Labour Ernest Bevin, these young men were randomly selected to work in coal mines instead of joining the armed forces. Their labor was critical to keeping Britain’s war industries running but earned little recognition at the time. Women were conscripted into services like the Auxiliary Territorial Service (ATS), where they served as clerks, drivers, mechanics and radar operators. Others joined the Women’s Royal Naval Service or the Air Transport Auxiliary. National Service also directed thousands into factories, farms, and shipyards, filling shortages in vital industries. Whether in uniform or overalls, conscripts formed the backbone of Britain's wartime manpower, helping sustain the war effort on all fronts."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'For_the_Queen'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/For_the_Queen.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Britain/Homefront/T_national__service.T_national__service","SubPathString":""},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/NATIONAL_SERVICE.NATIONAL_SERVICE","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":396.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_national_service_home_photo","SourceString":"Bevin Boys receiving training from an experienced miner at Ollerton, Nottinghamshire, UK, 1945.","LocalizedString":"Bevin Boys receiving training from an experienced miner at Ollerton, Nottinghamshire, UK, 1945."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""},"Notes":"Image needed to be customized","isDefenseBuff":true},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_national_service_home","owner":"card_event_national_service_home_C","params